    "result_text": "The full suite is green (BUILD SUCCESS).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented rejection of a future `registrationDate` on `POST /api/owners`, following the project's OfficeFloor function-injection pattern (not Spring MVC).\n\n**New files:**\n- `ValidateOwnerRegistrationDate.java` \u2014 a validation step that reads the published request body (`@Val OwnerFieldsDto`) and, when a `registrationDate` is supplied, throws if it is `isAfter(LocalDate.now())`. An absent date is accepted (it still defaults to the server date downstream).\n- `FutureRegistrationDateException.java` \u2014 a checked exception carrying the offending date.\n- `FutureRegistrationDateExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds **400** with `{\"errors\":[\"registrationDate\"]}`, matching the existing `InvalidPostcodeExceptionHandler` convention.\n- `officefloor/escalation/...FutureRegistrationDateException.yml` \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n\n**Wiring change:**\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted the `validateRegistrationDate` step between `validatePostcode` and `normalize`. It runs on the already-published body and *before* `DefaultOwnerRegistrationDate` (which would otherwise default/roll-forward the date), so validation sees the supplied value.\n\nThe new test `errorRejectsFutureDate` (posting `registrationDate: \"2999-01-01\"`) now returns 400, and all existing tests still pass.",
